course: dictionary
lesson: 156
title: Afrikaans, a language from South Africa
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Afrikaans is a language spoken in South Africa and Namibia. It grew from Dutch, which settlers brought to Africa in the 1600s, and it also took words from Malay, Portuguese and Khoekhoe. Languages mix like this when people from different places live and work together.
explain: You may already know some Afrikaans words without realising it. English took «aardvark», «trek» and «meerkat» from Afrikaans. Read the words around a new word, the context, to see what it means: «Duikers are small forest antelopes whose name means diver in Afrikaans» tells you the word «duiker» means diver.
explain: Some Afrikaans words change how English sounds in South Africa. In South African English, a «robot» is a traffic light, and «now now» means soon, while «just now» means some time later. «Lekker» means great or tasty. When you meet a word you do not know, check whether the sentence clues help before you look it up.
fact: Afrikaans is a language spoken in South Africa and Namibia.
fact: Afrikaans grew from Dutch, and it also took words from Malay, Portuguese and Khoekhoe.
fact: English took words from Afrikaans, such as «aardvark», «trek» and «meerkat».
fact: «Lekker» means great or tasty in South African English.
fact: «Duiker» is a small forest antelope, and its name means diver in Afrikaans.
exercise: What is Afrikaans? | A kind of antelope | A country in Africa | A type of traffic light | *A language spoken in South Africa and Namibia
exercise: «In South African English a "robot" is a traffic light.» What is a robot in this sentence? | A tall tree | *A traffic light | A kind of meerkat | A machine that walks
exercise: «Duikers are small forest antelopes whose name means diver in Afrikaans.» Why are they called duikers? | Because they dive into rivers | Because they live in the sea | *Because they plunge into thick bushes to escape | Because they swim fast
test: Which word did English take from Afrikaans? | Castle | *Trek | Harbour | Table
test: In South African English, what does «now now» mean? | Yesterday | Never | Some time ago | *Soon
exam: Afrikaans grew mostly from which language? | *Dutch | Khoekhoe | Portuguese | Malay
